    {
      "category": "short_to_ground",
      "components": ["D1"],
      "pins": ["D1 lower terminal"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "GND", "servo power rail"],
      "observed": "On page 1, D1's lower terminal connects to the servo power rail, and that rail's vertical wire continues to a GND symbol. D1's upper terminal is connected to +5V, creating a diode path from +5V to GND and grounding the servo supply rail.",
      "expected": "The servo power rail should be fed from +5V through D1 and remain separate from GND; the capacitors and servo power pins should connect to that rail, not to ground.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "D1 PMEG3030EP; +5V at its upper terminal; the lower-terminal wire runs to a GND symbol (page 1)."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "D2 | PMEG3030EP Schottky ... Diode from +5V into the servo power rail (page 1 of the specification)."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "C1–C4 | 100 µF × 4 | Bulk capacitance on the servo power rail (downstream of D2) (page 1 of the specification)."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The diode provides a conducting path from +5V to ground, likely causing excessive supply current when powered. The servo supply rail is grounded, so the servos will not receive the intended power.",
      "confidence": 0.97
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic connects D1's servo-rail output to GND, although the specification requires that diode to feed an isolated servo power rail. This creates a supply-to-ground current path and prevents the intended servo power delivery."
}

